diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 364056c..2e83dcc 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -2,6 +2,7 @@
 
 ## Unreleased
 
+- Fix RenderThread crashing (SIGSEGV with a garbage fault address, four times in 25 s in the sample) on a 120 Hz Android 16 / Mali phone when a page scrolled or a control was tapped. The device was auto-classified as Balanced, so its 60 Hz cap sent every glass surface through `GlassFrameCache`, where the glass layer's render node is reachable from the window and from a second `HardwareRenderer`; HWUI then used freed native state. Rendering capped glass off screen is now opt-in (`GlasmioPerformanceConfig(cachedGlass = true)`, `GlasmioPerformance.cachedGlass`). By default `frameRateCap` gates the glass clocks only, as in 0.2.1, so tier detection alone never enables the second renderer.
 - Fix an "Input dispatching timed out" ANR with `frameRateCap` set, typically when a menu opens. The capped-glass renderer draws into a 4-buffer queue, and buffers were only taken off that queue on the main thread. The RenderThread waited for a free buffer while drawing glass, the main thread waited for the RenderThread, and neither could continue. Finished glass images are now taken off the queue on their own thread (`GlasmioGlassFrames`). The main thread only swaps in the newest image, so the RenderThread never waits on it.
 - Fix the app crashing when a menu opens with `frameRateCap` set (0.2.2/0.2.3), and the backdrop going stale or blank with the cap on. While the window shows the cached glass image, the off-screen renderer is the only parent of the glass layer's render node. The cache destroyed that renderer on every size change (a menu resizes several times while it opens) and right after handing the layer back to the window. That dropped the node's last parent, and Android's renderer then deleted the glass layer's display lists while Compose still used them. Each cache now keeps one renderer for its whole life, a resize only swaps its output surface, and the renderer is destroyed only after the window has committed a frame that draws the glass layer itself.
 
diff --git a/docs/performance-tiers.md b/docs/performance-tiers.md
index 5247874..2ad7d22 100644
--- a/docs/performance-tiers.md
+++ b/docs/performance-tiers.md
@@ -39,7 +39,8 @@ The weaker of the SoC and RAM verdicts wins. Detection reads `Build.SOC_MANUFACT
 
 - **Backdrop downscale (`samplingScale`)** multiplies each surface's own effect-texture scale (floor 0.25×). Surfaces that already sample at 0.25–0.5× (buttons, tab bars, toolbars) are unaffected at the floor; full-resolution surfaces (navigation bar, pills, contact/toolbar/union shapes, menus, sheets, sidebars, backdrop blur) get cheaper. Shape fields (contact, toolbar, union) are evaluated in full-size pixels and mapped into the reduced effect space (`NativeGlassFilterField.atSamplingScale`), and the optical center is scaled with them, so downscaled glass keeps its size and position. Text, shape clips, highlights and shadows stay at device resolution. Where the control field plane needs unit scale, the renderer uses its analytic path, as it already did for any scale other than 1.
 - **Frame-rate cap (`frameRateCap`)** applies to the glass only; the window keeps drawing at the panel rate.
-  - **Glass rendering:** each backdrop surface renders its capture and filter chain (blur, lens, refraction) off screen (`GlassFrameCache`: its own `HardwareRenderer` into a hardware-buffer `ImageReader`) at most `frameRateCap` times a second. Every window frame in between composites the last image as a plain bitmap, so scrolling, text and the rest of the app stay at 120 Hz while the expensive glass work runs at 30 Hz. All capped surfaces in the process share one clock (`GlassFrameClock`): while any window with glass or a backdrop source draws an app frame, every surface re-renders at the cap, so the backdrop never freezes on an old frame. Glass also refreshes in popups over content that scrolls in another window. After the last app frame the clock runs 250 ms longer, then stops, so an idle screen does no glass work. Surfaces that export a backdrop, luminance-only probes, software-drawn views, wide-gamut (color-managed) windows and HDR headroom skip the cache and render live. A cap at or above the panel rate also renders live.
+  - **Off-screen rendering is opt-in (`cachedGlass = true`).** Without it the cap gates the glass clocks only and glass renders live, as in 0.2.1: the off-screen path below made RenderThread crash on a 120 Hz Mali phone.
+  - **Glass rendering (with `cachedGlass = true`):** each backdrop surface renders its capture and filter chain (blur, lens, refraction) off screen (`GlassFrameCache`: its own `HardwareRenderer` into a hardware-buffer `ImageReader`) at most `frameRateCap` times a second. Every window frame in between composites the last image as a plain bitmap, so scrolling, text and the rest of the app stay at 120 Hz while the expensive glass work runs at 30 Hz. All capped surfaces in the process share one clock (`GlassFrameClock`): while any window with glass or a backdrop source draws an app frame, every surface re-renders at the cap, so the backdrop never freezes on an old frame. Glass also refreshes in popups over content that scrolls in another window. After the last app frame the clock runs 250 ms longer, then stops, so an idle screen does no glass work. Surfaces that export a backdrop, luminance-only probes, software-drawn views, wide-gamut (color-managed) windows and HDR headroom skip the cache and render live. A cap at or above the panel rate also renders live.
   - **Glass animation clocks:** Glasmio's own animation clocks (navigation scaffold, adaptive navigation/sidebar, menu morph, loupe follow/spring, reaction bar) skip frames too. They integrate real elapsed time, so motion speed is unchanged.
   - Prefer divisors of the panel rate (30/40/60 on 120 Hz, 30 on 60 Hz).
 - **Field grid step** coarsens the CPU/FP16 liquid-menu field (4× fewer texels at 2×), which shrinks the per-update Bitmap allocation, native build and GPU upload. It takes effect at the next geometry update.
